<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating an Indefinite Integral by Partial Fractions 2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle\int{@question_expression@}dx\).</p>
<p><em>Note: First we use long division since the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator. By long division, we can identify the polynomial part and that of the remainder fraction. </em></p>
<p>Polynomial part: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]] </p>
<p>Remainder fraction part: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle\int{@question_expression@}dx=\) [[input:ans4]][[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To evaluate the following integral \(\displaystyle\int{@question_expression@}dx\), we need two consecutive steps.</p>
<p><strong><span>First step:</span> The integrand has to be expressed as a sum of its partial fractions.</strong></p>
<p>To write \( \displaystyle {@question_expression@} \) as a sum of its partial fractions we need to follow the steps below:</p>
<p>First use long division to write \( \displaystyle {@question_expression@}\) as a sum of a polynomial and a rational function. That is</p>
<p>\[{@question_expression@} ={@ta_3@}+{@expr5@}\].</p>
<p>Next is to write the rational function\( \displaystyle {@expr5@}\) as a sum of partial fractions. That is \( \displaystyle{@expr5@}={@part_frac_A@}+{@part_frac_B@}\) </p>
<p>Setting the numerators equal gives:</p>
<p>\[{@expr@}\]</p>
<p>Getting the values of A and B, we have to equate the coefficients of \(x\) and the constants in the equation above.</p>
<p>This should give \[{@expr5@}={@ta_1@}+{@ta_2@}\].</p>
<p>The partial fraction of the integrand is then,</p>
<p>\[{@question_expression@}={@ta_1a@}\]</p>
<p><strong>Second step: Evaluating the integral.</strong></p>
<p>From the step 1, we have:</p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle\int {@question_expression@}dx = \displaystyle\int{@ta_1@}\,dx +  \displaystyle\int{@ta_2@}\,dx + \displaystyle\int{@ta_3@}\,dx \]</p>
<p>\[ \iff \displaystyle\int {@question_expression@}dx = {@int_part1@}+ {@int_part2@}+ {@int_part3@}+c\]</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle\int {@question_expression@}dx = {@ta4@}\]</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
